title: Repo files reloaded
pid: 01a5070c-a527-4dc0-8295-ec2a1072efe4
body: |2-
    - Unprompted: the server sends this message without a request, when
      its repo watcher has reloaded =.skg= files that changed on disk
      ('server/repo_watch/'). It comes only between responses: a
      connection's loop sends it when no request, nor any part of one,
      has arrived for its read timeout, so it never falls inside another
      response. Both clients handle it whatever request they have in
      flight (Emacs's 'skg-unprompted-response-handler-map', Neovim's
      'state.unprompted_response_handler_map'); skg-for-ai prints its
      counts and warnings on standard error and reads on, and its daemon
      logs them and asks for the rerender as the clients do.
    - Message: LP =((response-type repo-files-reloaded) (reloaded ((SKGREPO N) ...)) (warnings ("WARNING" ...)) (view-ids (VIEW_ID ...)))=.
      =reloaded= names each skgrepo with files reloaded and how
      many, in declaration order. The warnings are one per node or batch
      that could not be reloaded, saying why and that the server keeps
      serving the previous version. A connection is not told of a
      skgrepo its skgrepo restriction keeps out, nor of a problem
      concerning one. The view IDs are the
      connection's open views that show a node the batch changed, and
      every buffer of the diff view it holds; every open view, with a
      warning saying so, when the connection fell too far behind to
      hear every batch. No message is sent when there is nothing to
      tell. Each buffer of the diff view among the view IDs is marked,
      when the connection has no skgrepo restriction, so that the next
      "Rerender all views" that includes it extends it as "Extend diff
      view" would ('docs/diff-view.org', "After a reload").
    - When there are no warnings, each client echoes the counts in one
      line ("skg: reloaded 2 files from repo first; reloaded 1 file from
      repo main"), in the echo area in Emacs, as a notification in
      Neovim. When there is a warning, it appends a line per count and
      per warning to its server-warnings buffer under a level-1 headline
      "Repo changes", as it does the startup warnings ("Server
      warnings"), and shows the buffer. Either way it
      asks for a rerender of each
      named view whose buffer has no unsaved edits: a "Rerender all
      views" request whose =exclude-view-ids= name every other open
      view. While another streaming request is in flight it asks again
      every half second, for up to a minute. A buffer with unsaved edits
      keeps them; its save is refused if it would write a node the
      batch changed (see "Save buffer", the stale buffer).
    - The watcher: the server watches every configured skgrepo
      directory, owned and foreign, with the platform's file-change
      notification (inotify on Linux), not recursively, since a
      skgrepo's =.skg= files sit directly in it. Once an event arrives it
      gathers events until the directories have been quiet for
      =repo_watch_quiet_ms= (see "skgconfig.toml"), then reloads the
      batch once, under the mutation gate, so never in the middle of a
      save: each changed node's telescope is read again from every
      skgrepo, validated as a save's nodeInstructions are, and swapped
      in as a new runtime generation, with the search index updated in
      the background; nothing is written to disk. A file that cannot be read
      leaves its node as the graph has it; a batch that fails validation
      leaves the whole graph as it was, and its files are tried again
      with the next change on disk. The server's own writes are
      recognized and dropped: each write or removal of a =.skg= file
      records the path and a hash of what it left there, and an event
      whose file still matches the record is the server's own. A
      changed config still needs "Rebuild session-lifetime stores"; the
      watcher follows the skgrepo directories of whatever config the
      server holds.
